    {
      "id": 16,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "title": "How to switch tasks · method credit",
      "pos": [-1320, 810],
      "size": [1720, 490],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 15,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## Three directed transformations\n\nKeep **primary image role** on `edit | strong scene anchor (FL2VA)` and **quality profile** on `directed change | 39-frame settle -> 1 image`. The node expands your instruction into a timed H3 I2VA prompt, finishes the change by 65%, holds the result, and the decoder chooses only among tail frames 34–38.\n\n- **Re-pose:** select `directed | re-pose character`; Picture 2 should show only the desired pose. Example: `Use <Picture 2> only for the target body pose: right hand raised, weight on left leg. Keep everything else from <Picture 1>.`\n- **Character swap:** select `directed | character swap`; Picture 2 should show the donor character. Example: `Replace the woman with the woman from <Picture 2>, including face, hair, physique, and wardrobe.`\n- **New camera angle:** select `directed | new camera angle`. Picture 2 is optional; disconnect the reference stack for a text-only move. Example: `Arc 45 degrees to camera right at the same height and focal length, preserving a medium shot.`\n\nChain more **Add H3 Edit Reference** nodes when a task needs more guides. Semantic guides are the recommended FL2VA route. Native guides can be mixed with the Picture 1 keyframe, but that combination is experimental.\n\n## Credit and inspiration\n\nThe controlled-motion-and-settled-frame method is inspired by **C_Nugget's** ingenious continuous-camera character-sheet workflow. Its disciplined assignment of motion to one controlled variable is adapted here for re-posing, character replacement, and camera movement.\n\n### [Open C_Nugget's original H3 Character Sheet Generator](https://huggingface.co/PoopMan333/H3_Character_Sheet_Generator)"
      ]
    }
  ],
